   Meh. Real buzzards probably wouldn't even be able to fly in a
dungeon --- no thermals. But it's a decent monster, so I wouldn't
be opposed to it. And it would provide more competition for food
in the early game.

> [10]: Make the buzzards only show up when multiple (5 or so) corpses of
> dead enemies exist on the level, and they leave spontaneously a random
> number of turns later (50-200 or so) after eating them all. While
> hallucinating, they appear as giant flying vacuum cleaners for the
> dungeon. --JoshJ

   You'd have to figure out how to make them "leave". The mail daemon
and the vault guard are the only NPCs who "leave" at the moment, and
they do it by moving uberquickly and mysteriously vanishing, respectively.
Neither really applies to buzzards.

> [13]: Burying your pet (or a dead human) should give you a substantial
> bonus to alignment and luck. Priests and other friendly humans should
> bury your corpse if you die (so that it's not just sitting under the
> ghost). If a priest does this, your ghost does not spawn; rather, the
> priest guards the bonespile. --TheShrewdDude

   Meh. Remember, "bury" in Nethack means "drop in a pit and roll a
boulder into it", which doesn't really correspond to human burial
rites. Also, it requires a boulder, which isn't available on demand
unless you waste a scroll of earth. So I don't see this getting used
much in real games.

